summary:
I believe it’s the next issue, #4, which will have Angus’ article, and it is a good one. So probably you should write them directly, as a start. There is a lot of material about me in Brian Aldiss’ BILLION YEAR SPREE, and also in his earlier THE SHAPE OF FUTURE THINGS. Books not yet published. FLOW MY TEARS, THE POLICEMAN SAID Doubleday this next spring, and then A SCANNER DARKLY, which is based on my experiences with persons whose brains were being burned out by drugs and didn’t know it. It is s-f. An experimental novel, CONFESSIONS OF A CRAP ARTIST, will be released by Elektra Records in about a year. I have some­ thing like 10 other experimental novels which no U.S. publisher would touch, the MSS over at the nearby archives of the University of California Fullerton; 
they vary in worth, but I am sure that if money were not the sole criterion here, at least three would have been print. I have considered sending them directly to French publishers—my agent agrees—because there it would seem that literary val­ ues play a greater role. 

What I see ahead for my writing is a deeper and deeper study of the ques­ tion, “What can we know? Much much is self-generated and projected, as one finds in direct brain stimulation, and is as real to the person as authentic objec­ tive reality. Also, so much information exists now in all areas that in a sense too much is known for anyone to make the right choice. If you put these two themes together (one) that in fact we know nothing about ou...
